Q10. "What is the difference between HTTP GET, POST, PUT, and PATCH, and which ones are idempotent?"
Answer:
- GET reads data without modifying server state (e.g., GET /api/menu). It is safe and idempotent.
- POST creates a brand-new resource (e.g., POST /api/orders). It is NOT idempotent—calling it twice creates two separate orders unless protected by an idempotency key.
- PUT replaces an entire resource completely. It is idempotent.
- PATCH partially updates specific fields of an existing resource (e.g., updating only item_status to 'Ready'). In Zaika, setting an item's status to 'Ready' multiple times produces the exact same state, making it idempotent.

